%I A399381 #49 Sep 17 2026 00:20:40
%S A399381 2,1,1,1,3,1,1,1,2,2,1,1,3,1,1,1,2,1,2,1,2,1,1,1,3,2,1,2,2,1,1,1,2,1,
%T A399381 1,1,2,1,1,1,3,1,1,1,2,2,1,1,2,3,2,1,2,1,2,1,2,1,1,1,3,1,1,2,2,1,1,1,
%U A399381 2,1,1,1,2,1,1,2,3,1,1,1,2,2,1,1,3,1,1,1,2,1,2,1,2,1,1,1,2,1,2,2
%N A399381 a(n) is the minimum positive integer k such that k*n-1 is squarefree.
%C A399381 For n > 1, a(n) >= A007424(n-1). In fact, a(n) = A007424(n-1) = 1 if n-1 is squarefree.
%C A399381 It appears that a(n+1) = A399382(n-1) for many values of n > 1. a(n+1) >= A399382(n-1) until a(26) = 2 < 3 = A399382(24).
%C A399381 From _Robert Israel_, Sep 07 2026: (Start)
%C A399381 a(n) always exists, in fact by Dirichlet's theorem there are infinitely many k for which k*n-1 is prime.
%C A399381 The sequence is unbounded.  For example, if p(1), ..., p(m) are distinct primes such that k is coprime to p(k) for k = 1..m, by the Chinese Remainder Theorem there is n such that k*n == 1 (mod p(k)^2) for k=1..m, and then a(n) > m. (End)
%C A399381 From _Aidan Markey_, Sep 13 2026: (Start)
%C A399381 If positive integers are selected at random, then the expected number of selections made before reaching a squarefree number is Pi^2/6 = 1.644934... (A013661). However, the average value of the first n terms of this sequence appears to converge to a constant less than Pi^2/6 as n grows arbitrarily large. For example, the average of the first 10^7 terms of this sequence is 1.4694863. This suggests that finding the smallest squarefree number of the form n*k-1 for a specific value of n (A399682) is, on average, more efficient than a random process.
%C A399381 I conjecture that the averages of the first n terms of this sequence and the first n terms of A399382 tend to the same constant as n grows arbitrarily large. (End)
%H A399381 Aidan Markey, <a href="/A399381/b399381.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A399381 Aidan Markey, <a href="/A399381/a399381.txt">Java program</a>
%e A399381 a(13) = 3 because 1*13-1 = 12 and 2*13-1 = 25 are not squarefree, but 3*13-1 = 38 is squarefree.
%p A399381 f:= proc(n) local k;
%p A399381    for k from 1 do
%p A399381      if NumberTheory:-IsSquareFree(k*n-1) then return k fi
%p A399381    od
%p A399381 end proc:
%p A399381 f(1):= 2:
%p A399381 map(f, [$1..100]); # _Robert Israel_, Sep 06 2026
%t A399381 A399381[n_] := NestWhile[# + 1 &, 1, !SquareFreeQ[#*n - 1] &];
%t A399381 Array[A399381, 100] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Sep 04 2026 *)
%o A399381 (Java) // see links section.
%o A399381 (PARI) a(n) = my(k=1); while (!issquarefree(k*n-1), k++); k; \\ _Michel Marcus_, Aug 28 2026
%o A399381 (Python)
%o A399381 from itertools import count
%o A399381 from sympy import factorint
%o A399381 def a399381(n):
%o A399381     if n < 3: return 3 - n
%o A399381     return next(k for k in count(1) if max(factorint(k*n-1).values()) < 2)
%o A399381 print([a399381(n) for n in range(1, 101)]) # _Michael S. Branicky_, Sep 07 2026
%Y A399381 Similar to A399382, A007424.
%Y A399381 Cf. A005117 (squarefree numbers), A008966.
%Y A399381 Cf. A399639 (index of the first occurrence of n in this sequence).
%Y A399381 Cf. A399682 (the resulting squarefrees a(n)*n-1).
